\subsection{The decision rule}{

Schurgin et al. (2020) model memory as a familiarity signal that spreads
from the target to other features according to their similarity: a
feature \code{x} steps away has mean familiarity \code{d * f(x)} and unit noise.
In continuous report the observer picks the strongest of many such
signals (\code{\link[=tcc]{tcc()}}). With a single probe there is one signal, and the
observer compares it with a criterion, as in yes/no signal detection.
The probability of answering "change" is

\deqn{P(\mathrm{change} \mid x) = \Phi(criterion - d f(x)).}{P(change | x) = Phi(criterion - d * f(x)).}

This is the likelihood of \code{\link[=sdt_yn]{sdt_yn()}} with equal variance, one trial per
row, and the probe's similarity in place of its 0/1 stimulus code: a
probe that matches the target is a signal trial, a probe with \code{f(x) = 0}
a noise trial. With changes of 180 degrees only, as in Williams et al.
(2022), \code{tcc_cd()} and \code{\link[=sdt_yn]{sdt_yn()}} fit the same model, with "same" as
"old". Probes at intermediate distances add operating points along \code{f}.
Schurgin et al. (2020) do not write this rule out; it is their
familiarity model with the decision stage of yes/no recognition. The
observer here knows nothing about the state of memory on a trial. For
the decision rule of Lin and Oberauer (2022), which weighs the
likelihood of a change, see the article on change-detection decision
rules on the package website.

\code{criterion} is measured from the familiarity of a probe with no
similarity to the target, \code{bias} from the midpoint between 0 and \code{d};
they are related by \code{bias = criterion - d / 2}. Write one of them in the
formula. A formula that names neither estimates \code{criterion ~ 1}; one that
names both is refused. The choice matters when \code{d} differs between rows
that share a criterion term, as described for \code{\link[=sdt_yn]{sdt_yn()}} in its section
"Choosing between \code{criterion} and \code{bias}".
}

\subsection{Similarity and distance}{

\code{similarity} is the vector \code{\link[=tcc]{tcc()}} takes: one value per distance in
steps, in the order 0, 1, ..., \code{n / 2}, starting with 1. Its length sets
the grid: 181 values are \code{n = 360} steps of 1 degree. With \code{units = "radians"}, \code{distance} is the probe minus the target in radians and is
assigned to the nearest step; with \code{units = "bins"} it is a whole number
of steps. Only the size of the distance matters, not its sign.
}

\subsection{Comparing \code{d} with \code{tcc()}}{

In the model both functions fit, \code{d} is one parameter, and data from both
tasks generated with the same \code{d} and \code{f} return the same value. On real
data, expect \code{tcc_cd()} to report the larger \code{d}. \code{\link[=tcc]{tcc()}} fits the
uncorrelated-noise model, which treats the 360 options of a colour wheel
as 360 independent signals. Schurgin et al. (2020, Extended Data Fig. 2)
report that only the correlated-noise d' is interchangeable with a d'
from a same/different task, and that the uncorrelated d' is that value
scaled by about 0.65. A single probe has no such count of channels, so
\code{tcc_cd()}'s \code{d} corresponds to their correlated d'. A joint fit of both
tasks that estimates one \code{d} therefore needs the scale factor as well.
}

\subsection{Set size and other predictors}{

Set size is not an argument: name its column in the formula, e.g.
\code{bmf(d ~ 0 + set_size, criterion ~ 1)}. \code{d} is on the log scale, as in
\code{\link[=tcc]{tcc()}}, so effects on it are multiplicative and priors on its
coefficients are priors on \code{log(d)}.
}

\subsection{Identification}{

A cell whose probes all have the same similarity gives one rate for two
parameters, so \code{d} and the criterion trade off. Every cell in which both
are estimated needs probes of at least two similarities, such as probes
at the target and probes far from it. \code{bmm()} warns when the whole data
set has only one.
}
}
